## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compliant, Azure-native data marketplace with per-tenant contracts, schema evolution, and privacy controls.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Data Lake Gen2, Purview, Schema Registry
- Per-tenant contracts, dynamic schema evolution
- Row-level masking, aggregated signals exposure
- Azure ML centralized model registry, BYOK, cross-region DR

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskRow(row, tenantId) {
  if (row.tenantId !== tenantId) return row;
  row.ssn = null;
  row.email = row.email ? row.email.replace(/^[^@]+@/, "***@"): row.email;
  return row;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement schema evolution without breaking existing contracts?
- What would you monitor to detect drift between contract expectations and ingested data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-preserving federated pipeline on Azure, balancing data residency, governance, and cost. It requires concrete components, data flow, and failure-mode reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning and secure aggregation in Azure Confidential Computing
- Tenant isolation via Delta Lake partitions on ADLS Gen2
- Central model registry in Azure ML with versioning
- Purview RBAC and BYOK for governance and encryption
- Cross-region DR and autoscale cost controls
- Latency targets and failure-mode handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator for federated updates
const globalModel = loadRegistry('federated/global/v1');
for (const tenant of tenants) {
  const upd = getTenantUpdate(tenant);
  const sealed = seal(upd);
  const agg = secureAggregate([sealed]);
  pushToRegistry(agg, 'federated/global/v1');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor drift and trigger retraining?
- How do you onboard/offboard tenants without affecting others?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical Azure data pipeline design with beginner-friendly constraints, testing real-world implementation of data isolation, infrastructure as code, and CI/CD practices.

## Key Concepts

- Event Hubs for scalable event ingestion
- Per-tenant data isolation through Azure SQL schemas
- PII filtering and transformation with Azure Functions
- Infrastructure as Code using Bicep
- Continuous integration/deployment with GitHub Actions
- Cross-region disaster recovery via SQL Geo-Replication and RA-GRS
- Cost optimization: autoscaling, partition configuration, retention policies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of routing logic for per-tenant data
function routeEvent(event){
  const tenantId = event.tenantId;
  // Filter PII and route to appropriate schema
  const filteredEvent = removePII(event);
  await writeToSQL(`tenant_${tenantId}`, filteredEvent);
}
```
